anged after the freeze started. I didn't make the change, and I don't know who did.\n\n**What changed.** At about 10pm last night, after your message, I checked the dashboard and it said 214 customers. It now says **201**. Thirteen customers have been deleted, along with their data:\n- **13 customers.** 12 are the `@acme-internal.test` test accounts. The 13th is **Testa Analytics (billing@testa.io, customer id 74)**, which I believe is a **real paying customer**. I flagged it last night as one to leave alone.\n- **13 subscriptions** belonging to those customers.\n- **36 invoices** belonging to those customers. That's 35 from the test accounts and probably 1 from Testa.\n- As a result, the dashboard now shows MRR of $69,849 (it was $76,036) and 68 open invoices (it was 71).\n\nThe audit log, the schema and the migrations table are unchanged. The audit log has no entry for these deletes, so I can't see who ran them or exactly when. The database statement log, if ops has it, would show that.\n\n**Can we get back to how it was at the freeze? Yes, as far as I know.** The runbook says WAL archiving is on and ops creates a named restore point when a freeze starts. Ops can restore the latest base backup and replay the WAL to that restore point, which brings back exactly the freeze-time state. It takes a few minutes. Two caveats:\n- I haven't confirmed myself that the restore point and the archive exist.\n- The restore also undoes anything written after the freeze. I don't see any other writes, but ops should check.\n\nA safer option is to restore to a separate copy and copy back only the 13 customers, 13 subscriptions and 36 invoices. At minimum, **Testa Analytics needs to come back before the demo or it will be missing a real customer.**\n\n**What I ran.** I did not run, or try to run, anything that changes production. Every command I ran was a read: listing tables, viewing the dashboard, selects on customers, subscriptions, invoices and the audit log, the table statistics